    {
      "id": "graph-organization-practical-composition-law",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Practical Composition Law — Working Notes",
      "position": {
        "x": 140,
        "y": 760
      },
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "width": 1620,
      "height": 620,
      "style": {
        "background": "linear-gradient(145deg, #111827 0%, #172554 52%, #0f3d3e 100%)",
        "color": "#f8fafc",
        "border": "2px solid #38bdf8",
        "borderRadius": 24,
        "boxShadow": "0 20px 54px rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.22)",
        "padding": 24
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Practical Composition Law — What Has Held Up\n\n> A graph composes safely when it exposes a stable, declared port whose selected surface can be consumed without exposing the graph's internal nodes, handles, authority, or storage arrangement.\n\n## 1. The public boundary is graph-level\n\nThe working chain is **declaration → exposed port → selected surface → portal**. A durable address may name a graph or one of its declared ports. It does not address a node handle; handles remain internal wiring.\n\n## 2. A port may delegate presentation\n\nA port is a stable boundary, not necessarily the card a visitor should see. It may remain `visible: false` while its surface selects a visible source node. Navigation resolves the port, then focuses and renders the selected source surface.\n\n## 3. Root is a routing convention\n\nA bare graph address and `:root` resolve the declared default exposure. Root does not require a special title node, and a single exposed port should not silently become root merely because it is alone. The declaration makes the choice explicit.\n\n## 4. Ownership stays with the source\n\nThe source graph owns its goal, state, content, derived summary, and updater. A workboard or other consumer owns only projection concerns such as lane, rank, pinning, and review time. Refresh refetches the materialized source view; it does not reconstruct or execute a missing source updater.\n\n## 5. Navigation is one-way by default\n\nA portal consuming a port establishes a directed navigation relationship. Bidirectional behavior and data return require an explicit agreement about schemas, lifetime, authority, failure, and security. They are capabilities layered onto composition, not assumptions hidden inside an edge.\n\n## 6. Infrastructure and meaning are different layers\n\nDeclarations, bridges, class bindings, ports, and updaters make a graph executable. They should be preserved as infrastructure without becoming the semantic hub of the graph. Authored edges should explain relationships between ideas; operational edges should appear only when they clarify a real operation, such as an updater materializing a summary.\n\n## 7. Templates must preserve machinery, not just appearance\n\nA derived graph must carry forward and retarget its declaration, bridges, bindings, ports, surfaces, updater, and handles. `dependencies.nodeTypes` describes requirements; it does not register classes or replace runtime authority.",
        "visibilityRole": "browser"
      },
